---
import Case from '../../layouts/Case.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import { projects } from '../../data/projects';
const project = projects.find(p => p.slug === 'pandora')!;
const chapters = [{id:'context',label:'Scope & contribution'}, {id:'workflow',label:'Execution contracts'}, {id:'incident',label:'Retries & concurrency'}, {id:'verification',label:'Operational verification'}, {id:'quality',label:'Quality & rollout'}, {id:'lessons',label:'Developer experience'}];
---
<Case slug="pandora" chapters={chapters}>
<section id="context"><p class="k">01 / Context</p><h2>Making agent execution inspectable.</h2><p>Pandora is my independent environment for AI-assisted engineering and operational automation. It connects durable task context, coding agents, integrations and execution records so work can be reviewed and continued across sessions.</p><div class="case-brief"><h3>My contribution</h3><p>I structure workflows, task constraints, acceptance criteria and integration requirements, and review and verify agent-assisted changes. Claude Code and Codex support implementation. The operational examples below describe documented work in this environment, rather than sole authorship of every underlying change.</p></div><p>The system is used in my personal engineering environment. The case distinguishes observed operation from tested components and planned capabilities.</p></section>
<section id="workflow"><p class="k">02 / Platform approach</p><h2>Define the task and the evidence together.</h2><p>A task needs more than a prompt: it needs scope, execution constraints and a definition of a verifiable result. Versioned Markdown preserves decisions; reviewable changes and execution records connect the instruction to what actually happened.</p><div class="architecture" aria-label="Agent-assisted task lifecycle"><div>Task context · Constraints · Acceptance criteria</div><span aria-hidden="true">↓</span><div>Agent-assisted implementation · Integration</div><span aria-hidden="true">↓</span><div>Review · Focused tests · Runtime verification</div></div><p>I use Claude Code and Codex for repository analysis, planning, code changes and documentation. The review loop is part of the workflow: a successful tool call is evidence of execution, while acceptance depends on the resulting behavior.</p></section>
<section id="incident"><p class="k">03 / Reliability case</p><h2>Two failure modes behind repeated MCP startup.</h2><p>An August 10, 2026 operational record documents gateway memory pressure alongside repeated startup of a payment-service MCP integration. Investigation identified two distinct problems: an invalid launcher generated continuous retries and child processes; concurrent discovery could create duplicate server instances before registration completed.</p>
<div class="outcome-grid"><div><h3>Launcher failure</h3><p>A compatible adapter using the stable application checkout replaced the missing launcher. The record reports no new MCP failures after that intervention in its observation window, without restarting the gateway.</p></div><div><h3>Discovery race</h3><p>The discovery filter considered registered servers, but did not reserve ownership for a connection still being established. Another discovery pass could start the same server again.</p></div></div>
<div class="case-decision"><h3>Treat “connecting” as an owned state</h3><p>The recorded correction made the in-progress connection registry count as active ownership. Discovery would no longer regard that server as available for another startup just because registration was incomplete.</p></div>
<div class="execution-comparison"><div><h3>Before</h3><ol><li>Discovery starts a server connection.</li><li>Registration is still incomplete.</li><li>A second discovery can start another instance.</li></ol></div><div><h3>After</h3><ol><li>A connection in progress counts as owned.</li><li>Discovery excludes that server from new startup.</li><li>Registration completes for the existing instance.</li></ol></div></div>
<p class="case-note">Simplified explanation of the documented discovery fix, not a complete concurrency algorithm. Duplicate MCP processes are distinct from duplicated payment transactions; this record establishes the former.</p>
<div class="evidence-table"><table><caption>Recorded verification, August 10, 2026</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Check</th><th scope="col">Observation</th><th scope="col">What it establishes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Discovery correction</th><td>Three focused tests passed.</td><td>Coverage of the corrected behavior in the focused suite.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Planned restart</th><td>Health recovered; one MCP instance was observed.</td><td>The change was activated and the intended process state was observed.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Restart verification</th><td>The first 90-second check expired; the service subsequently converged.</td><td>The verifier’s timing did not match the actual startup budget.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Timeout adjustment</th><td>Verification budget changed to 150 seconds; policy test passed.</td><td>The check was aligned with the service’s startup allowance.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Resource limits were applied as a separate containment measure. They bounded the process footprint; they did not replace the launcher and concurrency fixes. Long-term session and concurrency budgets remained follow-up work in the record.</p></section>
<section id="verification"><p class="k">04 / Observability</p><h2>Test the operation people depend on.</h2><p>A related incident exposed a different gap: trace ingestion continued, but Tempo searches returned HTTP 500 because the process referenced missing WAL blocks. A running container and successful ingestion were insufficient evidence that engineers could investigate an execution.</p><p>After the current WAL block completed, a restart was limited to Tempo. The recorded verification included a successful search returning five recent traces and retrieval of a trace by ID. Other observability services were left running.</p><div class="case-brief"><h3>Mitigation versus resolution</h3><p>Search was restored. The cause of the orphaned references and a dedicated search-failure probe remained unresolved. That distinction matters when deciding whether to close an incident or continue monitoring it.</p></div><p class="case-note">Sources: internal operational diary and Tempo incident record, August 10–11, 2026. These are historical observations, not a current uptime or reliability guarantee.</p></section>
<section id="quality"><p class="k">05 / Quality & rollout</p><h2>Keep quality evidence attached to execution.</h2><p>Pandora’s model-portfolio code includes post-execution quality evidence and task scoring. Its cost-sampling logic rejects incomplete usage records instead of treating unknown cost as zero, and distinguishes measured from estimated cost. This is a concrete accounting boundary, not proof of a complete production evaluation platform.</p><div class="outcome-grid"><div><h3>Implemented and testable</h3><p>Recorded orchestration foundations include typed task contracts, eligibility checks, capacity reservations, scheduling and circuit breakers.</p></div><div><h3>Activation is a separate claim</h3><p>The July implementation record explicitly placed those foundations outside the production execution path. Passing tests and publishing code did not establish production adoption.</p></div></div><p>For a shared agent platform, the next quality layer would be a versioned task dataset, explicit success and failure labels, and repeatable comparisons across prompt or model changes. That is a future validation direction here, not a claimed deployed capability.</p></section>
<section id="lessons"><p class="k">06 / Developer experience</p><h2>Make the next investigation easier.</h2><figure class="case-figure"><a href={project.image.src} aria-label="Open the Pandora Box console capture at full size"><Image src={project.image} alt={project.alt} widths={[600,1000,1600]} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 800px" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption>Pandora Box · Synthetic demonstration data. This console image is not evidence from the incidents above.</figcaption></figure><p>My product and frontend background shapes how I approach operational tools: make state understandable, keep contracts explicit, and help the next engineer find the relevant evidence. The value of a console is the investigation it enables.</p><p>These cases reinforced three practices: distinguish in-progress work from absent work, verify the operation rather than just process health, and keep mitigation, testing and rollout as separate states.</p><a class="text-link" href="/resume-operational-ai.pdf">Download agent workflows résumé <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></section>
</Case>
